<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of East Anglia</h1><p class="st-meta">520 CE – 869 CE (approx.) · capital Rendlesham</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the East Angles in Norfolk and Suffolk, famed for the Sutton Hoo ship burial and for its martyred last king, Edmund.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a> in the 5th century, and the Wuffingas dynasty, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuffa">Wuffa</a>, ruled by the 6th century. Its royal centre lay at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rendlesham">Rendlesham</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-deben">Deben</a> valley, close to the burial ground at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo">Sutton Hoo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raedwald-of-east-anglia">Raedwald</a>, overlord of the southern English around 616, probably lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo">Sutton Hoo</a> ship burial, with its gold and Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. He kept both a Christian altar and a pagan one. Later kings founded the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dommoc">Dommoc</a>, traditionally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunwich">Dunwich</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-of-burgundy">Felix</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-east-anglia">Anna</a>'s daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelthryth">Aethelthryth</a> founded the monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ely-cambridgeshire">Ely</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipswich">Ipswich</a> became one of the first post-Roman towns.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>East Anglia was often under Mercian overlordship in the 8th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-the-martyr">King Edmund</a> in 869 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> later settled the land as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/danish-kingdom-of-east-anglia">Danish Kingdom of East Anglia</a>. Edmund's cult grew at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bury-st-edmunds">Bury St Edmunds</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-east-anglia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of East Anglia" width="320" height="282"><figcaption>Image: Amitchell125 at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Williamson_p16_3.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>520 CE – 869 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rendlesham</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Raedwald, Anna, Edmund the Martyr</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglo-Saxon paganism; Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>East Anglia, Kingdom of the East Angles</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=694&amp;focus=kingdom-of-east-anglia" aria-label="Kingdom of East Anglia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4702 367 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of East Anglia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5022 623L4962 619"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4962 619h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5022 623h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5022 623h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=694&amp;focus=kingdom-of-east-anglia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
